         <h1>Price Informativeness</h1>
-        <p class="lede">Some investors pay a cost \(c\) to learn the payoff of a risky asset, and the others learn only from its price. Noise traders, investors who trade for reasons unrelated to the payoff, add a random supply of the asset with standard deviation \(\sigma\), the noise volume, so the price moves for reasons unrelated to the payoff. With the fraction of investors who pay to learn held fixed, more noise makes the price reveal less about the payoff; in the model here, on the interior where that fraction lies strictly between 0 and 1, at a fixed cost the fraction rises with the noise, and there the price's informativeness, the squared correlation of the payoff and the price, does not depend on the noise volume. You write the solver that finds the fraction of investors who pay to learn (the informed fraction, \(\lambda\)), and you report how that fraction and the price's informativeness move with the noise and with the cost of information.</p>
